TEXAS REENTRY STIPEND PILOT
A Proposed Research Study

Invest $7,600 to Save $75,000
Breaking the Cycle of Recidivism Through Strategic Financial Investment

Submitted by: The Collaborative Advocate Foundation (TCAF)
EIN: 41-3618003
17912 Stefano Drive, Pflugerville, TX 78660
Principal Investigator: Dr. Terry Flood, DHA
Contact: mr.terryflood@gmail.com

------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

Texas releases 60,000 people from TDCJ annually with $50 and a bus ticket. Nearly half -- 46.1% -- return within three years. Each re-incarceration costs taxpayers $25,174 per year, totaling $75,000 or more per person over a typical 3-year sentence. This pilot tests whether strategic financial investment at the moment of release -- combined with workforce development incentives -- can fundamentally change that trajectory.

The Texas Reentry Stipend Pilot is a 4-arm randomized controlled trial with 400 participants (100 per arm), conducted across Travis and Williamson counties over 42 months. The study measures the dose-response relationship between financial support at release and subsequent recidivism, employment, housing stability, and community reintegration.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. THE PROBLEM

2.1 Day 1 After Release
On the day a person is released from TDCJ, they receive:
-- $50 in cash (unchanged since 1973)
-- A bus ticket to the county of conviction
-- No housing secured
-- No job lined up
-- Often an expired ID
-- No phone or ability to contact employers/family

$50 does not cover a single night in a motel in any Texas metro area. The average motel room in Austin costs $89/night.

2.2 The First 90 Days
Without support, the spiral begins immediately:
-- 33% of released individuals experience homelessness within 60 days
-- 60% remain unemployed at 90 days post-release
-- 68% relapse into substance use within the first year
-- The median time to employment for formerly incarcerated individuals is 8.2 months

2.3 The Cost of Failure
When recidivism occurs, the cost to Texas taxpayers is staggering:
-- $25,174 per inmate per year in incarceration costs
-- $68.97 per inmate per day
-- 46.1% three-year recidivism rate in Texas
-- Estimated 27,660 people return to prison annually
-- Total annual cost of recidivism: approximately $696 million

------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. THE INTERVENTION: 4-ARM STUDY DESIGN

3.1 Control Group -- Status Quo ($50)
Current TDCJ practice: $50 cash and a bus ticket home.
Per-person investment: $50
Sample size: 100 participants

3.2 Arm 1 -- Cash Stability ($250)
$250 at release. Tests whether immediate cash stability alone changes reoffending behavior. No other supports beyond what is normally available.
Per-person investment: $250
Sample size: 100 participants

3.3 Arm 2 -- Enhanced Cash ($500)
$500 at release. Tests dose-response: does more immediate cash produce a larger effect? This amount is closer to what might cover 5-7 days of basic expenses.
Per-person investment: $500
Sample size: 100 participants

3.4 Arm 3 -- Full Bridge ($7,600)
A comprehensive financial bridge package:
-- $500 at release (immediate stabilization)
-- $500 workforce enrollment bonus (within 14 days)
-- $3,600 paid training ($15/hr, 20hrs/wk, 12 weeks)
-- $3,000 post-program stipend ($500/mo for 6 months)
Per-person investment: $7,600
Sample size: 100 participants

This arm tests the full hypothesis: when you bridge every financial gap that drives recidivism -- immediate cash, workforce connection, training income, and sustained support -- can you fundamentally change the trajectory?

------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. WHAT WE MEASURE

4.1 Primary Outcomes (12, 24, 36 months)
-- Rearrest rates
-- Reconviction rates
-- Reincarceration rates

4.2 Secondary Outcomes
-- Employment status and income at 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36 months
-- Housing stability (consecutive months housed)
-- Substance use treatment engagement
-- Emergency department and crisis service utilization
-- Family reunification indicators

4.3 Community-Level Outcomes
-- Local crime rates in participant ZIP codes
-- Economic activity in participant neighborhoods
-- Community services utilization patterns

------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. COST-BENEFIT ANALYSIS

5.1 Pilot Scale (400 participants)
If the Full Bridge arm reduces recidivism by 20%:
-- Investment per participant: $7,600
-- Savings per diverted re-incarceration: $75,522 (3 years at $25,174/yr)
-- Net savings per diverted person: $67,922
-- At 20% reduction: 20 fewer incarcerations from 100 participants
-- Total pilot savings: $1,358,440
-- Total pilot investment: $760,000 (100 participants x $7,600)
-- Net benefit: $598,440
-- Return on Investment: 179%

5.2 State Scale (60,000 annual releases)
If applied to all TDCJ releases with similar effect:
-- Annual investment: $456 million (60,000 x $7,600)
-- Annual savings at 20% reduction: $2.27 billion (12,000 fewer incarcerations)
-- Net annual savings: $1.81 billion
-- The intervention costs just 13.9% of the incarceration budget

5.3 The Key Insight
Even at a conservative 5% recidivism reduction, the program pays for itself. At 15% or above, it becomes one of the highest-ROI investments the state can make.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. TIMELINE -- 42 MONTHS

Phase 1: Design and IRB (Months 1-6)
-- IRB approval and study protocol finalization
-- Partnership agreements with Travis County Sheriff, Williamson County, TDCJ
-- Randomization methodology and data collection systems
-- Staff hiring and training

Phase 2: Enrollment (Months 7-18)
-- Active enrollment of 400 participants across both counties
-- Baseline data collection
-- Intervention delivery (arms 1-3)
-- Monthly data collection for active participants

Phase 3: Follow-Up (Months 19-36)
-- 12-month follow-up data collection
-- 24-month follow-up begins for early enrollees
-- Interim analysis and preliminary findings
-- Community impact assessment

Phase 4: Analysis and Dissemination (Months 37-42)
-- Full 36-month follow-up for early cohort
-- Statistical analysis and manuscript preparation
-- Policy brief for 90th Texas Legislature (2027 session)
-- Dissemination to DOJ, NIJ, and state corrections agencies

------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. PILOT SITES

7.1 Travis County (Primary Site)
-- 4,200 annual TDCJ releases
-- Existing partnerships: Travis County Sheriff, Austin Transitional Center, Goodwill Central Texas, Integral Care
-- Data access: Austin Police Department, ECHO (homelessness data), Capital Area Workforce Solutions

7.2 Williamson County (Secondary Site)
-- 1,100 annual TDCJ releases
-- Growing suburban reentry population
-- Rapid population growth creating new service gaps
-- Partnerships: Williamson County Community Supervision, Opportunities for Williamson and Burnet Counties

------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. FUNDING STRATEGY

This pilot is designed to attract funding from multiple aligned sources:

8.1 Arnold Ventures (Primary Target)
-- Major criminal justice research funder
-- Focus on evidence-based policy reform
-- Has funded similar dose-response and RCT studies
-- Typical award: $250K-$2M for rigorous research

8.2 National Institute of Justice (NIJ)
-- Federal criminal justice research agency
-- Annual solicitations for reentry research
-- Focus on what works in reducing recidivism
-- Typical award: $250K-$750K

8.3 DOJ Second Chance Act
-- Federal funding specifically for reentry programs
-- Emphasis on evidence-building and outcomes measurement
-- Competitive grants for innovative approaches

8.4 MacArthur Foundation -- Safety and Justice Challenge
-- Focus on reducing over-incarceration
-- Interest in alternatives that demonstrate cost savings
-- Systems-change approach aligns with pilot design

8.5 Texas Legislature -- 90th Session (2027)
-- Policy brief timed for legislative session
-- Data-driven argument for reforming the $50 gate money statute
-- Bipartisan appeal: fiscal conservatism meets public safety

------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. WHY TCAF

The Collaborative Advocate Foundation operates a 24-platform AI-powered operating system for communities. The following platforms directly support this research:

-- RPLICE: Implementation science engine providing CFIR/RE-AIM frameworks, fidelity measurement, and evidence-practice bridge
-- Justice Command Center: Criminal justice data aggregation across BJS, FBI UCR, TDCJ, and local law enforcement
-- Mission Transition (M2C): Veteran and civilian career transition platform with workforce pathway mapping
-- Workforce Dashboard: Employment tracking, skills gap analysis, and labor market integration
-- LifeBridge: Benefits screening and enrollment for SNAP, Medicaid, CHIP, WIC, EITC
-- ISSS: Integrated support coordination with early warning systems and multi-stakeholder case management

TCAF is not proposing this from theory. Every measurement tool, data pipeline, and coordination infrastructure already exists and is currently operational.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

10. DATA SOURCES

Federal: Bureau of Justice Statistics (BJS) recidivism data, FBI Uniform Crime Report, Census ACS, CDC PLACES, CDC Social Vulnerability Index, Bureau of Labor Statistics, SAMHSA, HUD

State: TDCJ Offender Data, Texas Workforce Commission (TWC), Office of Court Administration (OCA), HHSC Benefits Data

Local: Travis County Sheriff Office, Austin Police Department, ECHO Homelessness Data, Capital Area Workforce Solutions, Integral Care, Central Health

------------------------------------------------------------------------

11. CLOSING

The question is not whether we can afford this program.
The question is whether we can afford not to.

Every person who cycles back through the prison system costs Texas taxpayers $75,000 or more. Every person who successfully reintegrates becomes a taxpayer, a parent, a neighbor, and a contributor.

The difference between those two outcomes may be as simple as having enough money to survive the first 90 days.

This pilot will prove it -- or disprove it -- with the rigor that Texas policy deserves.

